    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d71">

                <pb facs="0188" n="76" xml:id="pg76"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102S/1343: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-03-11T15:00:00+08:00">March 11, 1935—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received March 11—7:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>113. Legation’s 109, March 7, 5 p.m., regarding Shanghai defense scheme.
                    Consulate General reports that question of consultative consular representation
                    has been taken up orally without definite results. Both matters will receive
                    further attention upon return to Shanghai of Colonel Beaumont<anchor n="61"
                        xml:id="d71-f1"/> who is now in Peiping.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d71-f1" n="61" xml:id="d71-f1-content">
                <p>Col. John C. Beaumont, commanding the 4th U. S. Marine Regiment at Shanghai.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
